> ## 1. Map Design: What the Dungeon Looks Like
> 
> You don’t need a precise grid; you want **clear areas** that reveal nicely as “fog of war.” Use the zones we already created:
> 
> - **A. Mine Entrance & Guard Tunnels**  
>   - Wooden-framed entrance, warning signs, tunnel with side alcove.  
> - **B. Collapsed Shaft & Side Passages**  
>   - Vertical shaft with broken lift platform.  
>   - Rubble blocking main way.  
>   - Narrow side tunnel slanting down.  
>   - Small side passage where miner spirit appears.  
> - **C. Crystal Cavern (Rest Area)**  
>   - Big round-ish chamber, glowing crystals, pool, old bedrolls.  
> - **D. Howling Chasm & Bridges**  
>   - Huge vertical crack cutting across the map.  
>   - One broken mine-track bridge, one rope bridge.  
>   - Tunnel on far side that leads down.  
> - **E. Titan Chamber**  
>   - Large circular chamber.  
>   - Central glowing pedestal with Titan Scale.  
>   - A couple of stone pillars/rock formations.
> 
> **Topology (very rough):**
> 
> - Entrance (A) → straight tunnel → collapsed shaft (B, middle of map).  
> - From B you get two downward paths:  
>   - Fix the lift → straight down to C.  
>   - Side tunnel → curves into C.  
> - From C, one main tunnel to D (the chasm).  
> - From D, cross the chasm → tunnel to E (Titan Chamber).
> 
> Think of it as a “bent dumbbell”:
> 
> A → B → C → D → E  
> …with a chasm between D-left and D-right.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. How to Reveal the Dungeon at the Table
> 
> ### Low-friction physical method (highly recommended)
> 
> Even if you generate the art with AI, the **reveal** is easiest if you:
> 
> 1. Print the map at A4/Letter.  
> 2. Put a full sheet of **plain paper on top**, cut into hinged flaps:
>    - One flap covering A, one for B, one for C, one for D, one for E.  
> 3. As he reaches each zone, you **fold back that flap**.
> 
> If you want more granularity, you can cut the cover sheet into more irregular shapes to match tunnels, but the zone-level reveal is probably ideal for a 6-year-old.
> 
> You can do this with a single **master map**, so you only need one good image.
> 
> If you really want **digital reveal**:
> - Toss the image into **Foundry / Roll20 / Owlbear Rodeo** and just use black rectangles you delete, or a fog-of-war brush.
> - Or even a simple PowerPoint/Keynote file with black shapes you delete on-click.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Workflow A: DALL·E – Single Master Map
> 
> DALL·E is not great at **precise layout control**, but for a zone-based “hand-drawn fantasy map” it can be enough.
> 
> ### Prompt for the full dungeon map
> 
> Paste this as-is, then refine if needed:
> 
> > A clean, kid-friendly hand‑drawn fantasy dungeon map for a tabletop RPG.  
> > Top-down view, no perspective, no grid.  
> > The dungeon is called “The Howling Crack Mine.”  
> > Style: simple line art with light shading, like a fantasy children’s book map, clearly readable, not cluttered, no blood or gore.  
> > Show these labeled areas:  
> > 1. “A. Mine Entrance and Guard Tunnels” – a wooden-framed mine entrance with warning signs, leading into a simple tunnel and a small side alcove.  
> > 2. “B. Collapsed Shaft and Side Passages” – a vertical mine shaft with a broken lift platform, piles of rubble blocking a main passage, and a narrow sloping side tunnel. Include a small side room off a tunnel, where a ghostly miner might appear.  
> > 3. “C. Crystal Cavern (Safe Rest)” – a larger rounded cavern filled with glowing crystal clusters and a small underground pool, maybe with rolled-up bedrolls or crates.  
> > 4. “D. The Howling Chasm and Bridges” – a deep crack or chasm cutting across the mine. Show one broken mine track bridge and one rope bridge crossing the chasm. There should be tunnels leading to and from the chasm.  
> > 5. “E. Titan Chamber” – a big circular final chamber reached after crossing the chasm. In the center, draw a stone pedestal with a glowing crystal “Titan Scale.” Add a few stone pillars or rock formations.  
> > The overall layout should roughly flow from A to B to C to D to E in a loose path, with connecting tunnels clearly drawn. Leave the background blank or very lightly textured stone. Use clear labels and simple decorative touches but keep it easy to read.
> 
> If DALL·E doesn’t label nicely, you can add the letters by hand after printing.
> 
> ### Using it with reveal
> 
> Once you have one map you like:
> 
> - Print it.  
> - Use the paper-flap method to reveal.  
> 
> If you want multiple “reveal stages” digitally from DALL·E (less reliable, more work):
> - Ask DALL·E to generate **cropped/inset versions** like “Only show area A and a bit of tunnel toward B,” etc., but consistency between images may be annoying.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. Workflow B: Stable Diffusion / AUTOMATIC1111 (My preferred suggestion)
> 
> Given your preference for SD WebUI, I’d do:
> 
> 1. **Hand-sketch the map layout** on paper or in a drawing app.  
> 2. Scan or photo it → use **img2img** to beautify it into a consistent style.  
> 3. Then print that result for physical reveal.
> 
> This way **you control the topology** exactly.
